# Code consistency

Readers skim code by pattern. When similar things look and work alike, the next person
understands a new file in seconds; when one module does something differently, every
reader has to stop and ask whether the difference matters. Match what is already there.
Change a convention on purpose, in its own change — never by drift.

## Look before you write

Before adding code to a module, open two or three sibling files of the same kind (same
folder, same role: handler, component, store, spec) and copy their shape: file layout,
export style, ordering, error handling, how they are tested. Search for an existing
helper before writing one — cross-package utilities live in `shared/` (import via `@shared`).

```ts
// ❌ BAD — a new handler in its own style: inline Firestore access, console.log, ad-hoc response
export const apiRemoveRepo = onRequest(async (request, response) => {
  const doc = await getFirestore().collection('repositories').doc(request.body.id).get();
  if (!doc.exists) {
    response.json({ ok: false });
    return;
  }
  console.log('removing', doc.id);
  await doc.ref.delete();
  response.json({ ok: true });
});

// ✅ GOOD — same shape as the sibling handlers: thin handler → domain function → store
export const apiRemoveRepo = onRequest({ invoker: 'public' }, async (request, response) => {
  try {
    await removeRepo(request.body);
    response.status(204).send();
  } catch (error) {
    logError('Failed to remove repository', { error });
    response.status(400).send({ error: 'Invalid repository payload' });
  }
});
```

## One way per problem

Use what the project already uses before adding something new: its validation library,
test runner, path aliases, logging helper, and error style. A new dependency or pattern
needs a reason the existing one cannot meet; state it in the PR description.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — a second way to validate input next to the schema pattern used elsewhere
if (typeof input.name !== 'string' || input.name === '') throw new Error('bad name');

// ✅ GOOD — the boundary pattern the repo already uses
const item = itemSchema.parse(input);
```

## Let the tools format

Formatting is decided by `.prettierrc` and `.editorconfig`, and lint rules by
`eslint.config.js`. Run the formatter instead of hand-formatting, and do not reformat
lines you did not change: incidental reformatting buries the real change in the diff.

## Existing conventions win until changed on purpose

If an existing pattern looks wrong, do not mix a "better" one into one corner of the
code. Either follow the current pattern, or propose changing it for the whole area as a
separate, focused change (record architectural choices as an ADR in
`wiki/architectural-decision-records/`). A consistent imperfect module is easier to
fix later than a patchwork of styles. If the neighbors disagree with each other, follow
the most widely used pattern and mention the inconsistency.

## Quick check before finishing

1. Does the new code look like its neighbors?
2. Did I reuse the existing helper, library and alias instead of adding another?
3. Does the diff contain only lines I meant to change?

If any answer is no, fix it before you hand the change over.
